#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69107280-70c4-5ca4-8b64-1e64450165b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448278} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3f6955-4016-59d3-9db9-3955354a9810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a3f6955-4016-59d3-9db9-3955354a9810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448279} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ee137d6-1832-58d0-85d5-575e29a0fa26" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449179} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29bde20c-189c-54ef-b19c-9e8950c65826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448280} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9158da-7221-5175-b881-fdd36f8f42ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29bde20c-189c-54ef-b19c-9e8950c65826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448280} :answer-field/id #uuid "de466e8f-4f63-50c6-b72f-38c520f95211" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449180} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6c45389-9701-5934-b1ac-2f11edac2800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448282} {:answer/id #uuid "f20f2e0e-7487-58ee-9feb-810b44ca227a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f20f2e0e-7487-58ee-9feb-810b44ca227a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448283} :answer-field/id #uuid "db9964be-6910-5d74-bd0f-7b3cfcffc16d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "364a18ef-2de8-57d7-9e2f-631eb4651446" :question/math-academy-id "q-127931" :question/problem "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n- **III.** The lines $x+3y=-1$ and $-x+ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k+3\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to  $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124479 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1073953e-7e0a-5237-8533-8882b7772741" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13016ec0-8156-589e-b39b-942c97ca1edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263181} {:answer/id #uuid "4ace33b3-69d8-5761-a1dd-efead09fb2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263642} {:answer/id #uuid "60dec4b1-c098-5349-b8d9-90a530ef64b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263835} {:answer/id #uuid "9087593d-b85f-524d-b8a4-0f0def706f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264242} {:answer/id #uuid "b50e48ba-06d3-5a6a-927a-0e46fa9eaae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ace33b3-69d8-5761-a1dd-efead09fb2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263642} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9aa03e6-7fbd-5806-a04e-b966abc7083c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06c2ebc8-9d9b-5ee7-87a8-a42672daac19" :question/math-academy-id "q-130958" :question/problem "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $D$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -4 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $D$ has $3$ pivots, and therefore the number of pivot columns is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02e65c73-8c02-5403-8c5c-b1e81f81a7b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With Many Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "564a9b9e-aab5-5b0f-93e8-ff25aa13561b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336826} {:answer/id #uuid "74d85b4a-b12a-5c0f-b5ed-ff52586806e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337084} {:answer/id #uuid "a8927049-3f50-5f32-81d8-ae0adb9041c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337555} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b2cb1f-b916-5d6a-83bd-0b171941131c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337754} {:answer/id #uuid "f8db2c98-cfca-5e9d-8c6d-040c9d9da0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8927049-3f50-5f32-81d8-ae0adb9041c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337555} :answer-field/id #uuid "21e0fd22-373d-5dc1-ac92-23af629fc5c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e129d90-7991-5aeb-8e6d-b77b70757df7" :question/math-academy-id "q-69982" :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $4$th column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(Q) & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1)^{1+4}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =-5\\cdot (2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3)=-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(Q)=-30.\n$$"}]}
